<h2>The two columns</h2>
<p><strong>Who you are</strong> is the side, four columns of the twelve, pinned under the bar: the photograph, the wordmark and the name, the role in one line, whether you are free, how to reach you, and the blocks — skills as bars, tools as chips, certifications and languages as rows with the year or the level on the right. <strong>The career</strong> is the main: four numbers at a glance, then numbered sections — summary, experience, education, selected projects (the repo cards), talks and writing, what people said.</p>
<p>A role is one row: the dates on the left in the mono face (the current one carries the dot), the title, the company and place, three achievements with a number in each, and the stack as chips. Three bullets, not seven — a recruiter reads the first and skims the rest.</p>
